15:38.820 --> 15:39.320
G by M.

15:39.500 --> 15:39.900
Exactly.

15:40.260 --> 15:42.840
Another for how the physical environment interacts with the microbiome.

15:42.880 --> 15:43.360
G by M.

15:43.660 --> 15:49.540
And finally, a massive three-way interaction term for a genotype by environment by microbiome.

15:49.600 --> 15:50.920
It's like a mathematical web.

15:50.920 --> 15:56.860
It explicitly forces the math to acknowledge that microbial composition is a structured,

15:57.120 --> 15:59.900
interacting, non-random component of the whole system.

16:00.160 --> 16:00.400
Okay.

16:00.560 --> 16:06.480
So, you know, from a layperson's perspective, that seems like a pretty clean, logical solution.

16:06.900 --> 16:08.860
We found a missing piece of the puzzle.

16:09.200 --> 16:11.900
We updated the mathematical equation to include it.

16:12.180 --> 16:13.020
Problem solved.

16:13.400 --> 16:14.260
We can all go home, right?

16:14.740 --> 16:16.120
If only it were that simple.

16:16.380 --> 16:16.660
Right.

16:16.660 --> 16:21.780
Because as you read further into Fliction's essay, you realize that moving a variable out

16:21.780 --> 16:25.540
of a statistical junk drawer seems like an easy fix only in hindsight.

16:26.480 --> 16:28.880
It raises this massive historical question.

16:29.440 --> 16:34.000
Why did it take agricultural science, with all its funding, all its brilliant scientists,

16:34.820 --> 16:37.020
why did it take them decades to do this?

16:37.080 --> 16:38.780
It's the multi-billion dollar question.

16:38.780 --> 16:39.140
Right.

16:39.340 --> 16:43.200
I mean, we've known microscopic life exists in dirt for a very, very long time.

16:43.400 --> 16:43.800
We have.

16:44.120 --> 16:47.060
And that historical delay is the true crux of the essay.

16:47.220 --> 16:47.460
Okay.

16:47.860 --> 16:50.840
Because the lag wasn't due to a lack of biological knowledge.

16:51.080 --> 16:57.160
Plant breeders and soil scientists in the 1970s and 80s knew perfectly well about soil microbiology.

16:57.280 --> 16:57.540
Yeah.

16:57.620 --> 16:58.800
I learned about it in grade school.

16:59.200 --> 17:02.720
They knew about nitrogen-fixing bacteria in legumes, for instance.

17:02.720 --> 17:08.980
So the reason it took so long to formally incorporate the microbiome into the mathematical models

17:08.980 --> 17:13.100
wasn't just a problem of missing data or weak computers.

17:13.480 --> 17:14.040
What was it then?

17:14.240 --> 17:21.120
It was because of a fundamental, almost philosophical misunderstanding of what a plant actually is.

17:21.280 --> 17:21.760
Oh, wow.

17:21.880 --> 17:22.040
Okay.

17:22.060 --> 17:23.440
This transitions us perfectly.

17:23.720 --> 17:26.360
Because it's not just an epistemological problem, right?

17:26.440 --> 17:28.880
A problem of how we measure reality.

17:29.060 --> 17:29.280
Right.

17:29.280 --> 17:33.840
It's an ontological problem, a problem of what reality fundamentally is.

17:34.100 --> 17:34.500
Precisely.

17:35.160 --> 17:39.340
The essay dives into a section called The Blurry Boundaries of Life, and I'm not going

17:39.340 --> 17:41.220
to lie, this completely broke my brain.

17:41.320 --> 17:42.300
It's a paradigm shift.

17:42.380 --> 17:45.620
It really challenged how I view the entire natural world.

17:45.760 --> 17:51.140
To understand why the math was stuck for so long, we have to look at the biological concept

17:51.140 --> 17:53.300
of the holobiont or the meadoworganism.

17:53.840 --> 17:54.440
Holobiont.

17:54.540 --> 17:54.820
Yes.

17:54.820 --> 18:00.080
Fliction references the foundational work of biologists and theorists like Scott Gilbert,

18:00.540 --> 18:04.780
Jan Sapp, and Alfred Tauber, as well as Thomas Bosch and Margaret McFolmenguy.

18:04.880 --> 18:05.920
What's their main argument?

18:06.380 --> 18:11.120
The argument they've been building over the last couple of decades is genuinely revolutionary.

18:11.120 --> 18:18.040
They argue that the individual organism, the bounded, discrete entity we intuitively think

18:18.040 --> 18:24.480
of as a plant or an animal or a human, is not actually a fundamental independent unit

18:24.480 --> 18:25.180
of biology.

18:25.260 --> 18:25.740
Wait, hold on.

18:25.940 --> 18:26.820
Let's pause there.

18:26.900 --> 18:31.180
Because I feel like every biology class I ever took from middle school onward taught me that

18:31.180 --> 18:33.960
the individual organism is the fundamental unit of life.

18:34.260 --> 18:34.700
Of course.

18:34.700 --> 18:40.600
You study the frog, you dissect the earthworm, you measure the bean plant, it's always about

18:40.600 --> 18:41.180
the individual.

18:41.520 --> 18:46.900
And we are deeply, deeply conditioned to see the world as a collection of discrete objects

18:46.900 --> 18:47.420
like that.

18:47.760 --> 18:49.320
So how are they defining it instead?

18:50.080 --> 18:55.400
These researchers argue that an organism is much more accurately understood as a symbiotic

18:55.400 --> 18:55.960
assemblage.

18:56.080 --> 18:57.240
A symbiotic assemblage?

18:57.260 --> 18:57.520
Yes.

18:58.080 --> 19:03.660
Its core physiological functions, its immune system, its behavioral traits, and its very anatomical

19:03.660 --> 19:04.100
development.

19:04.280 --> 19:04.520
Yeah.

19:04.680 --> 19:09.200
They're all constituted through ongoing, inseparable, real-time interactions with its

19:09.200 --> 19:10.960
associated microbial communities.

19:11.160 --> 19:11.340
Mm.

19:11.600 --> 19:16.460
And the essay brings up this point about how the microbiome is partially heritable, which

19:16.460 --> 19:17.560
is the part that really threw me.

19:17.680 --> 19:19.140
It's counterintuitive at first.

19:19.480 --> 19:19.920
Very.

19:20.540 --> 19:25.500
Because when I picture a plant getting a microbiome, I just picture it sitting in the dirt and whatever

19:25.500 --> 19:29.720
bacteria happen to wash over it in the rain are the bacteria it gets.

19:29.840 --> 19:29.960
Right.

19:29.960 --> 19:33.800
Like, you know, getting dust on your shoes, you just happen to walk through a dusty room.

19:34.220 --> 19:36.760
But it's far more active and intentional than that.

19:37.420 --> 19:41.340
The genetic code of the plant dictates its physical root architecture.

19:41.920 --> 19:47.280
So how deep the taproot goes, the density of its fine root hairs, things like that.

19:47.320 --> 19:48.240
Okay, that makes sense.

19:48.300 --> 19:48.920
That's genetics.

19:49.180 --> 19:54.580
But more importantly, the plant's genetics dictate the precise chemistry of its root exudates.

19:54.960 --> 19:56.080
Root exudates.

19:56.080 --> 19:58.780
Let's dig into the mechanics of that because it's fascinating.

19:59.440 --> 20:01.160
What exactly is the plant doing?

20:01.720 --> 20:07.800
Plants literally secrete complex cocktails of chemicals into the soil surrounding their

20:07.800 --> 20:08.140
roots.

20:08.320 --> 20:09.860
Just pumping it out into the dirt.

20:10.020 --> 20:10.540
Constantly.

20:10.920 --> 20:17.840
We are talking about a highly specific mixture of sugars, amino acids, organic acids like malic

20:17.840 --> 20:20.080
or citric acid, and secondary metabolites.

20:20.700 --> 20:22.140
And this is the crazy part.

20:22.140 --> 20:28.720
A plant can expend up to 30% of the carbon it fixes during photosynthesis just to produce

20:28.720 --> 20:29.620
these exudates.

20:29.920 --> 20:30.420
Wait, really?

20:30.620 --> 20:31.140
30%?

20:31.200 --> 20:32.360
Up to 30%, yes.

20:32.540 --> 20:36.880
That is a massive amount of metabolic energy to just pump out into the dirt.

20:37.060 --> 20:42.740
I mean, nature doesn't waste energy like that unless it has a very, very specific purpose.

20:43.080 --> 20:43.660
And it does.

20:44.060 --> 20:46.860
The purpose is chemical signaling and cultivation.

20:47.100 --> 20:47.700
Cultivation.

20:47.960 --> 20:48.240
Yes.

20:48.520 --> 20:51.720
The plant is actively brewing a chemical summons.

20:52.140 --> 20:57.960
It is purposefully attracting, feeding, and recruiting specific microbial guilds from

20:57.960 --> 21:00.500
the surrounding bulk soil to come live on its roots.

21:00.760 --> 21:01.300
Oh my god.

21:01.340 --> 21:06.520
Different plant genotypes will systematically assemble distinctly different microbial communities

21:06.520 --> 21:10.080
even if you plant them in the exact same homogenized soil.

21:10.340 --> 21:13.580
So it's not just an environment the plant is randomly subjected to.

21:13.700 --> 21:14.180
Not at all.

21:14.300 --> 21:17.140
The microbiome is an extended phenotype of the plant.

21:17.140 --> 21:22.360
The plant's genes are literally expressing themselves outside of its own physical body

21:22.360 --> 21:26.000
out into the soil to build a customized support system.

21:26.140 --> 21:26.500
Yes.

21:27.140 --> 21:28.380
It's farming the microbes.

21:28.440 --> 21:29.420
That is unbelievable.

21:29.420 --> 21:37.940
And if we accept that the plant's genetics purposefully build the microbiome, we hit a profound

21:37.940 --> 21:41.660
logical wall when we try to use the old G by E model.

21:42.060 --> 21:42.280
Okay.

21:42.380 --> 21:44.300
I need to ask a clarifying question here.

21:44.400 --> 21:44.580
Right.

21:44.580 --> 21:49.280
Because I feel like my brain is starting to tie itself in knots trying to draw a circle around

21:49.280 --> 21:49.700
the plant.

21:49.820 --> 21:50.120
Go ahead.

21:50.120 --> 21:54.240
If the plant's internal genetics purposefully build the external microbiome.

21:54.480 --> 21:54.560
Right.

21:54.740 --> 21:59.360
But that external microbiome then turns around and dictates the plant's internal immunity,

21:59.860 --> 22:03.420
its nutrient uptake, and its ability to survive a drought.

22:03.660 --> 22:03.920
Yes.

22:04.100 --> 22:06.480
Where does the plant end and the soil begin?

22:06.820 --> 22:08.760
This raises an important question, doesn't it?

22:08.760 --> 22:09.660
A huge one.

22:09.740 --> 22:14.160
And that is the exact paradox that completely broke the classical modeling framework.

22:14.760 --> 22:19.600
If the plant is partly constituted by the microbes, it actively recruits.

22:19.600 --> 22:20.000
Yeah.

22:20.300 --> 22:25.120
And the microbiome is partly constituted by the chemical exudates of the plant that recruits

22:25.120 --> 22:25.220
it.

22:25.300 --> 22:25.440
Right.

22:25.760 --> 22:30.780
Then the boundary between the organism and its environment is not a clean biological line.

22:30.880 --> 22:31.720
It's completely blurred.

22:31.920 --> 22:33.380
It is a reciprocal entanglement.

22:33.640 --> 22:35.860
Neither entity is fully independent of the other.

22:36.000 --> 22:37.080
So they can't be separated.

22:37.460 --> 22:37.700
No.

22:38.040 --> 22:44.040
The plant is not an independent subject that simply possesses a microbiome, you know, the

22:44.040 --> 22:46.260
way you or I might possess a jacket or a tool.

22:46.320 --> 22:47.180
It's not an accessory.

22:47.660 --> 22:48.060
Exactly.

22:48.060 --> 22:50.140
They co-constitute each other's reality.

22:50.460 --> 22:56.540
So if the biological reality of the world is this incredibly blurry, entangled, symbiotic,

22:56.760 --> 23:03.620
hollow-bient mess, why does science insist on drawing a hard mathematical box around

23:03.620 --> 23:04.140
the plant?

23:04.240 --> 23:05.340
It's a great question.

23:05.520 --> 23:08.980
Like, why do we take a pair of scissors, cut the roots off at the soil line, weigh the

23:08.980 --> 23:12.980
top half, and confidently declare this is the unit we are measuring?

23:12.980 --> 23:19.040
Well, to unpack why human institutions do this, fliction actually turns away from biology

23:19.040 --> 23:21.220
entirely and turns toward philosophy.

23:21.540 --> 23:21.640
Right.

23:21.640 --> 23:27.600
Specifically, the essay brings in the work of the 20th century French philosophers Gilles

23:27.600 --> 23:29.720
Deleuze and Félix Guattari.

23:30.120 --> 23:31.140
Deleuze and Guattari.

23:31.180 --> 23:31.380
Yes.

23:31.460 --> 23:34.840
As interpreted for social theory by a sociologist named William Bogart.

23:34.920 --> 23:39.080
I have to admit, when I was reading a paper about crop yields and suddenly hit a section

23:39.080 --> 23:42.280
on post-structuralist French philosophy, I was intimidated.

23:42.280 --> 23:44.240
It's a sharp left turn, for sure.

23:44.500 --> 23:44.720
Yeah.

23:45.320 --> 23:50.140
Usually, that kind of philosophy can feel very abstract and disconnected from reality.

23:50.660 --> 23:55.980
But the way fliction uses it to explain statistical models is surprisingly practical.

23:56.740 --> 24:02.660
Deleuze and Guattari actually provide the perfect conceptual vocabulary for what went wrong in

24:02.660 --> 24:03.780
agricultural science.

24:03.800 --> 24:04.120
Okay.

24:04.320 --> 24:04.840
Let's hear it.

24:04.840 --> 24:10.600
They distinguish between two fundamental levels of organization in the world, or two ways of

24:10.600 --> 24:11.800
perceiving reality.

24:12.280 --> 24:14.340
The molar and the molecular.

24:14.560 --> 24:15.980
Molar and molecular.

24:16.260 --> 24:20.240
These sound like chemistry terms, but you're saying they are using them philosophically.

24:20.360 --> 24:20.760
Exactly.

24:20.900 --> 24:22.220
Let's break those down slowly.

24:22.360 --> 24:23.000
What do they mean?

24:23.240 --> 24:24.180
Let's start with the molecular.

24:24.780 --> 24:29.800
Think of molecular processes as the primary underlying fluid realities of the world.

24:30.120 --> 24:31.020
Fluid realities.

24:31.120 --> 24:31.280
Right.

24:31.380 --> 24:37.640
These are the flows, the raw forces, the continuous connections, the micro-interactions that basically

24:37.640 --> 24:38.820
never stop moving.

24:38.820 --> 24:42.760
Can we anchor that with a non-plan example first, just to get the vibe of it?

24:42.800 --> 24:42.980
Sure.

24:43.180 --> 24:43.680
Think of a city.

24:43.760 --> 24:44.000
Okay.

24:44.200 --> 24:44.580
A city.

24:44.840 --> 24:49.800
The molecular reality of city is the millions of individual conversations happening on the

24:49.800 --> 24:50.080
street.

24:50.520 --> 24:52.820
It's the flow of electricity through the grid.

24:53.040 --> 24:53.660
The traffic.

24:53.660 --> 24:54.140
Yes.

24:54.240 --> 24:56.100
The movement of money and cash registers.

24:56.260 --> 24:56.440
Yeah.

24:56.660 --> 24:59.300
The smell of food wafting out of a restaurant window.

24:59.620 --> 25:02.400
It's chaotic, it's dynamic, and it's continuous.

25:02.820 --> 25:04.360
You can't put a box around that.

25:04.460 --> 25:04.700
No.

25:04.800 --> 25:05.820
It's constantly shifting.

25:06.280 --> 25:07.900
Now, apply that to agriculture.

25:08.720 --> 25:13.720
The molecular reality of the field consists of those chemical exudate flows we just talked

25:13.720 --> 25:13.980
about.

25:13.980 --> 25:15.720
The 30% carbon tax.

25:15.820 --> 25:16.300
Exactly.

25:16.580 --> 25:22.000
The nutrient exchanges, the microscopic fungal networks pulsing with phosphorus, the real-time

25:22.000 --> 25:25.940
energy transfers between a tiny root hair and a single bacterium.

25:26.040 --> 25:28.680
So it's a continuous dynamic web of relations.

25:28.940 --> 25:30.900
It's the messy, entangled reality.

25:30.900 --> 25:35.300
It's the hollow biont actually doing its thing in the dirt, completely oblivious to our

25:35.300 --> 25:36.120
attempt to measure it.

25:36.120 --> 25:36.240
Okay.

25:36.320 --> 25:37.020
I get the molecular.

25:37.280 --> 25:38.240
It's the raw flow.

25:38.360 --> 25:39.260
So what is the molar?

25:39.780 --> 25:42.320
Contrast that fluid reality with molar segments.

25:42.820 --> 25:47.700
Molar segments are the rigid, large-scale, bounded categories that human beings impose on the

25:47.700 --> 25:51.200
world to make it manageable, measurable, and institutional.

25:51.560 --> 25:52.820
Ah, the boxes.

25:53.180 --> 25:53.480
Yes.

25:53.680 --> 25:56.860
The plant, the soil, the species, the genotype.

25:57.040 --> 25:57.340
Okay.

25:57.340 --> 26:00.140
In our city example, the molar segment is the zip code.

26:00.480 --> 26:01.700
It's the city limit sign.

26:01.800 --> 26:02.980
It's the census data.

26:03.560 --> 26:04.320
That makes perfect sense.

26:04.420 --> 26:11.380
So the molecular is the raw flow of reality, and the molar is the conceptual box we draw around

26:11.380 --> 26:12.960
it so we can put it in a spreadsheet.

26:13.400 --> 26:13.780
Precisely.

26:14.200 --> 26:19.160
And it is crucial to note here that Deleuze and Guattari aren't saying molar segments are

26:19.160 --> 26:21.360
inherently bad or that they are fake.

26:21.620 --> 26:22.640
We need zip codes.

26:23.120 --> 26:24.320
We absolutely need them.

26:24.380 --> 26:24.520
Whoa.

26:24.800 --> 26:28.040
They are entirely necessary for human cognition and society.

26:28.760 --> 26:35.020
A molar segment is a temporary crystallization or a stabilization of those underlying molecular

26:35.020 --> 26:35.580
dynamics.

26:35.680 --> 26:35.960
Okay.

26:35.960 --> 26:40.080
I mean, the physical molar plant you can hold in your hand is a crystallization of all

26:40.080 --> 26:41.180
those microscopic flows.

26:41.740 --> 26:46.920
But a massive problem arises when we forget that the molar segment is derived from the

26:46.920 --> 26:47.700
molecular flows.

26:47.780 --> 26:48.820
When we mix them up.

26:48.880 --> 26:49.160
Yes.

26:49.740 --> 26:54.120
We start treating the molar segment as if it were the primary, fundamental, independent

26:54.120 --> 26:54.720
reality.

26:55.020 --> 26:58.320
We fall in love with the map and forget that the territory is what actually matters.

26:58.460 --> 26:58.800
Exactly.

26:59.100 --> 27:03.300
And this leads to a sociological concept Bogart calls, over-coding.

27:03.520 --> 27:03.820
Over-coding.

27:03.820 --> 27:04.120
Yes.

27:04.720 --> 27:10.200
When a conceptual system like the G by E plant breeding framework achieves massive institutional

27:10.200 --> 27:12.100
success, it becomes rigid.

27:12.540 --> 27:14.180
Because everyone uses it.

27:14.260 --> 27:14.540
Right.

27:15.100 --> 27:21.460
Remember, G by E generated unprecedented returns on investment for decades during the Green Revolution.

27:21.860 --> 27:25.660
It drastically improved crop yields at a time when we really needed it.

27:25.740 --> 27:26.560
It saved lives.

27:26.560 --> 27:27.200
It did.

27:27.500 --> 27:31.120
But because it worked so well initially, it became deeply embedded.

27:31.120 --> 27:33.920
It was coded into university curricula.

27:34.120 --> 27:35.380
It was what you learned in grad school.

27:35.620 --> 27:36.040
Exactly.

27:36.540 --> 27:38.300
It dictated government funding structures.

27:38.620 --> 27:42.600
It was hardwired into statistical software packages used by everyone.

27:42.760 --> 27:44.740
So it just became the truth.

27:44.880 --> 27:45.180
Yes.

27:45.620 --> 27:50.380
Because the framework was so socially and economically successful, agricultural institutions

27:50.380 --> 27:52.580
began to over-code reality.

27:52.840 --> 27:53.300
Wow.

27:53.300 --> 27:59.100
They treated the molar plant, the bounded individual organism perfectly divorced from the soil, as

27:59.100 --> 28:01.280
the absolute natural unit of reality.

28:01.380 --> 28:05.840
They saturated the concept of the individual plant with so much institutional importance

28:05.840 --> 28:07.620
that they simply couldn't see anything else.

28:08.000 --> 28:12.560
They imposed their mathematical boundaries as if they were the natural physical boundaries

28:12.560 --> 28:14.540
of the phenomena themselves.

28:14.940 --> 28:16.340
Which we just learned they aren't.

28:16.340 --> 28:16.800
Right.

28:17.120 --> 28:23.660
And in doing so, they completely blinded themselves to the molecular microbiome that was actually

28:23.660 --> 28:26.840
doing a massive portion of the heavy biological lifting.

28:26.900 --> 28:27.760
That is wild.

28:27.980 --> 28:34.360
The G by E framework actively produced the individual organism as the only valid unit of selection.

28:35.020 --> 28:40.080
If you try to talk about selecting for an ecological network, the math simply wouldn't let you.

28:40.440 --> 28:41.400
It didn't compute.

28:41.580 --> 28:41.920
Literally.

28:41.920 --> 28:46.700
It made it conceptually impossible to see the ecological assemblage that was actually driving

28:46.700 --> 28:47.260
the yield.

28:47.660 --> 28:52.320
This is such a profound insight into how science works and, you know, the text connects it to

28:52.320 --> 28:56.220
something that I think makes it highly relatable, even if you have zero interest in farming.

28:56.460 --> 28:58.020
Yeah, the analogies in the paper are great.

28:58.200 --> 29:04.160
Fliction compares this molar blindness in agriculture to exactly what happens in the field of economics.

29:04.640 --> 29:06.440
The parallel to economics is striking.

29:07.200 --> 29:11.420
And it illuminates exactly how over-coding functions in human society.

29:11.420 --> 29:13.560
Okay, think about classical economics.

29:14.560 --> 29:19.540
In economic models, the molar unit, the rigid category they use to make the math balance,

29:19.840 --> 29:22.720
is the individual household or the corporate firm.

29:23.040 --> 29:23.160
Right.

29:23.360 --> 29:27.100
And the ultimate metric of reality is market price.

29:27.940 --> 29:31.260
If a transaction has a price tag, it exists in the model.

29:31.940 --> 29:34.020
If it doesn't have a price tag, it doesn't exist.

29:34.120 --> 29:34.740
It's invisible.

29:34.740 --> 29:35.380
Exactly.

29:36.000 --> 29:42.060
But as feminist economists like Marilyn Waring pointed out so brilliantly back in the 1980s,

29:42.100 --> 29:46.880
because the entire macroeconomic model is organized exclusively around market price,

29:47.220 --> 29:51.020
it completely ignores the massive reality of unpaid domestic labor.

29:51.140 --> 29:51.300
Yeah.

29:51.520 --> 29:56.720
The labor of raising children, maintaining a functional home, elder care, community building.

29:56.780 --> 29:56.980
Right.

29:57.080 --> 30:00.040
These are massive foundational economic forces.

30:00.400 --> 30:01.960
The labor is incredibly real.

30:01.960 --> 30:04.300
It consumes millions and millions of hours.

30:04.540 --> 30:05.400
It's exhausting work.

30:05.620 --> 30:07.120
And it is economically consequential.

30:07.220 --> 30:09.900
I mean, if you pull all unpaid domestic labor out of a country,

30:10.300 --> 30:13.840
the formal economy literally collapses overnight because workers can't go to their jobs.

30:13.920 --> 30:14.780
The system breaks down.

30:15.040 --> 30:18.620
But because that labor doesn't have a formal market price attached to it,

30:18.900 --> 30:22.320
it doesn't fit into the molar segment of the economic model.

30:22.460 --> 30:23.720
So it's left out of the equation.

30:23.940 --> 30:24.280
Completely.

30:24.280 --> 30:29.340
When economists calculate the gross domestic product, that labor is completely invisible.

30:29.680 --> 30:33.380
It's just treated as background noise or a free natural resource.

30:33.680 --> 30:35.960
It's the exact same mechanism of exclusion.

30:36.280 --> 30:36.420
Right.

30:36.820 --> 30:42.280
The economists ignored domestic labor for the exact same reason the plant breeders ignored the microbiome.

30:43.260 --> 30:47.700
Because the mathematical tools they built didn't have a pre-existing category for it.

30:47.860 --> 30:53.000
It's a perfect illustration of how a molar framework dictates what is visible to us.

30:53.000 --> 30:59.220
And Flickfea also brings up cognitive science as another really compelling example of this phenomenon.

30:59.560 --> 30:59.960
Oh, yeah.

31:00.020 --> 31:01.120
The brain in the jar.

31:01.360 --> 31:01.540
Right.

31:02.080 --> 31:07.020
For decades, the biological brain was treated as the isolated molar unit of cognition.

31:07.880 --> 31:09.920
The brain is the computer doing the thinking.

31:10.360 --> 31:13.480
And the body is just the meat vehicle carrying the computer around.

31:13.900 --> 31:18.080
And the surrounding environment is just the stuff the vehicle occasionally bumps into.

31:18.500 --> 31:19.000
Exactly.

31:19.000 --> 31:32.020
And that over-coding caused cognitive scientists to absorb all the incredibly complex ways our physical bodies, our tools, and our environments actively shape our thinking and just toss them out.

31:32.080 --> 31:33.300
Like into the junk drawer.

31:33.600 --> 31:33.900
Yes.

31:34.280 --> 31:44.380
If someone solves a math problem better because they are allowed to use their hands to gesture while they think, the old model just categorized that as a context effect or put it in the error term.

31:44.480 --> 31:46.880
Because it wasn't happening purely inside the skull.

31:46.880 --> 31:54.800
Right. Only recently, with theories of embodied and extended cognition, are we realizing that the mind leaks out into the world.

31:54.960 --> 31:55.720
It leaks out.

31:55.980 --> 32:00.460
Thinking happens in the interaction between the brain, the body, and the notebook you are writing in.

32:00.840 --> 32:04.720
The mind leaks out into the world, just like the plant leaks out into the soil.

32:04.860 --> 32:06.220
That is...wow.

32:06.460 --> 32:09.780
So, to you listening right now, take a second to think about how wild this is.

32:09.860 --> 32:10.480
It really is.

32:10.480 --> 32:15.500
We structure our entire modern world around these mathematical and conceptual models.

32:15.760 --> 32:21.500
We use them to grow our food, to measure the health of our economies, to understand our own minds and medical treatments.

32:21.660 --> 32:22.860
We rely on them for everything.

32:23.200 --> 32:23.600
Everything.

32:23.600 --> 32:32.360
But this essay is pointing out how incredibly often we mistake the boundaries of our tools for the actual boundaries of reality.

32:32.680 --> 32:34.180
We confuse the map for the territory.

32:34.320 --> 32:34.520
Right.

32:34.800 --> 32:42.100
We draw a conceptual box around a plant or a market transaction or a human brain simply because it's convenient for our math.

32:42.760 --> 32:46.780
And then, over time, we trick ourselves into believing that the box is real.

32:46.780 --> 32:49.300
And that everything outside the box is just random noise.

32:49.500 --> 32:49.900
Exactly.

32:50.180 --> 32:55.160
And that realization leads perfectly into the next logical question, which is perhaps the most practical one.

32:55.240 --> 32:55.880
How do we fix it?

32:55.960 --> 32:56.600
Basically, yeah.

32:56.820 --> 33:00.820
How do we actually know when our molar models are failing us?

33:01.040 --> 33:05.200
When the box is no longer serving us but actively holding us back?

33:05.280 --> 33:05.580
Right.

33:05.820 --> 33:08.540
Because, as you mentioned earlier, the noise isn't always random.

33:08.820 --> 33:13.380
The answer is, we have to train ourselves to look for the patterns in the noise.

33:13.380 --> 33:13.820
Yes.

33:13.960 --> 33:16.680
Let's dive into the difference between noise and structure.

33:17.060 --> 33:27.100
Because the residuals in that classical G by E equation, the mountains of deity left in that epsilon junk drawer, they weren't just chaotic static fuzz.

33:27.180 --> 33:28.200
They were trying to tell a story.

33:28.580 --> 33:29.000
They were.

33:29.680 --> 33:34.720
And to clarify this point, Flip Sheen brings in the work of the Nobel laureate Daniel Kahneman.

33:35.140 --> 33:36.860
The behavioral economics guy?

33:36.860 --> 33:37.420
Yes.

33:37.880 --> 33:44.540
Specifically, his later work with Olivier Sibony and Cass Sunstein on the concept of noise in human judgment.

33:44.840 --> 33:45.000
Okay.

33:45.000 --> 33:53.300
Kahneman makes a very distinct, very important separation between two concepts that we often conflate, bias and noise.

33:53.540 --> 33:54.920
Let's separate those out.

33:55.060 --> 33:55.980
What is the difference?

33:56.200 --> 33:58.900
Think of a team of people shooting at a target with rifles.

33:58.900 --> 33:59.220
Okay.

33:59.480 --> 34:00.180
Target practice.

34:00.520 --> 34:04.860
Bias is a systematic deviation in a consistent, predictable direction.

34:05.340 --> 34:10.300
If all the shooters hit the top left corner of the target tightly clustered together, that's bias.

34:10.460 --> 34:12.480
They are all making the exact same mistake.

34:12.580 --> 34:12.780
Right.

34:13.340 --> 34:16.000
The sights on the rifles are misaligned in the exact same way.

34:16.040 --> 34:16.920
It's predictable error.

34:17.100 --> 34:17.480
Got it.

34:17.740 --> 34:18.640
So what is noise?

34:18.960 --> 34:25.660
Noise, as conventionally understood in statistics, is just unwanted random variability that the model leaves unexplained.

34:25.660 --> 34:34.500
If the shooter's bullets are scattered completely randomly all over the target, above, below, left, right of the bullseye with absolutely no discernible pattern, that's noise.

34:34.720 --> 34:36.460
It's the scatter on the graph.

34:36.820 --> 34:39.460
It doesn't point you toward a specific solution.

34:39.720 --> 34:40.480
It's just messy.

34:40.480 --> 34:53.320
But Kahneman's deeper lesson, which flixion applies directly to these biological models, is that what appears to be random noise to a flawed model might actually be the signature of hidden structure.

34:53.600 --> 34:55.480
The noise is actually a signal.

34:55.640 --> 34:56.040
Exactly.

34:56.440 --> 35:00.180
For example, Kahneman talks about judges handing down criminal sentences.

35:00.500 --> 35:01.540
Oh, I've heard this one.

35:01.700 --> 35:02.480
It's fascinating.

35:02.860 --> 35:06.860
If you look at the sentences in aggregate, they might look randomly noisy.

35:06.860 --> 35:11.400
You have different judges giving wildly different sentences for the exact same crime.

35:11.500 --> 35:12.640
Looks like random scatter.

35:13.400 --> 35:16.940
But if you analyze the noise more closely, you find structure.

35:17.420 --> 35:24.680
You find that judges give significantly harsher sentences right before lunch when they are hungry, and more lenient sentences right after they've eaten.

35:24.820 --> 35:26.120
That is terrifying, by the way.

35:26.320 --> 35:26.780
It is.

35:27.080 --> 35:29.300
But mathematically, the noise wasn't random.

35:29.720 --> 35:32.560
It was structured by an unmeasured variable blood sugar.

35:32.940 --> 35:33.900
Unmeasured structure.

35:33.900 --> 35:36.600
Okay, so how does that map onto the plant breeders?

35:36.860 --> 35:46.780
In the plant world, breeders were seeing genotypes fail to replicate their performance across different environments in ways the G by E model simply couldn't explain.

35:47.000 --> 35:47.140
Right.

35:47.420 --> 35:54.300
A genetic strain would yield great in one state and terribly in another, but the model adamantly said the environments were identical.

35:54.840 --> 35:57.720
The heritability estimates were jumping all over the place.

35:57.760 --> 35:59.980
It looked like judges giving random sentences.

35:59.980 --> 36:00.600
Exactly.

36:01.000 --> 36:10.040
What looked like irreducible, frustrating, random noise across thousands of field trials was actually the hidden, elegant structure of biogeography.

36:10.160 --> 36:11.760
It was the microbial communities.

36:11.860 --> 36:12.080
Yes.

36:12.840 --> 36:18.680
Varying systematically by soil type, land history, and geography, practically screaming to be noticed.

36:18.800 --> 36:22.620
The residuals had developed recognizable spatial organization.

36:22.620 --> 36:22.940
Right.

36:23.340 --> 36:25.300
The scattered points on the graph weren't random.

36:25.660 --> 36:29.060
They were forming a literal shadow of the microbiome across the landscape.

36:29.940 --> 36:32.700
The junk drawer was vibrating with a specific frequency.

36:33.260 --> 36:39.240
And this leads to a semantic distinction in the text that I think is absolutely crucial for understanding the history of science here.

36:39.340 --> 36:40.360
Very important point.

36:40.580 --> 36:46.620
The source text explicitly emphasizes that the microbiome was not discovered by plant breeders in the 2020s.

36:46.620 --> 36:48.240
It was recovered.

36:48.480 --> 36:49.120
Recovered.

36:49.280 --> 36:49.520
Yes.

36:49.720 --> 36:53.620
That is a very precise and highly intentional word choice by the author.

36:53.660 --> 36:54.400
Why does that matter?

36:54.800 --> 37:04.940
Because a discovery implies finding something entirely new, something previously completely unknown to human knowledge, like discovering a new galaxy with a telescope.

37:05.260 --> 37:05.480
Right.

37:05.580 --> 37:07.240
Something that wasn't there before.

37:07.240 --> 37:16.520
But a recovery is the recognition and formal integration of something that was always there, that was even known to exist anecdotally or in other disciplines.

37:16.620 --> 37:16.840
Yeah.

37:17.100 --> 37:23.880
But was systematically excluded from formal mathematical representation by the architecture of the dominant framework itself.

37:24.000 --> 37:24.240
Right.

37:24.320 --> 37:30.160
It's not like a plant breeder in 1995 looked through a microscope and shouted, my God, there are tiny bugs in the dirt.

37:30.300 --> 37:30.780
Exactly.

37:31.360 --> 37:32.800
They knew soil microbes existed.

37:33.340 --> 37:35.040
Everyone knew about nitrogen fixation.

37:35.040 --> 37:42.720
But their statistical framework, the only tool they had to measure success, had literally no vocabulary to include them.

37:42.720 --> 37:51.320
Because the model only had variables for genotype and environment, breeders could not select for a plant's ability to recruit a good microbiome.

37:51.480 --> 37:52.480
It just didn't compute.

37:53.140 --> 37:55.020
That trait was invisible to their math.

37:55.400 --> 38:02.560
They couldn't identify a plant whose high yield was entirely dependent on a specific soil microbe rather than its own genetic superiority.

38:02.560 --> 38:11.800
Because the framework forced them to treat the microbe as just a generic, interchangeable environmental background factor, like the rain.

38:12.060 --> 38:12.380
Yes.

38:12.900 --> 38:22.760
Flixion draws another amazing parallel here to economics to really drive this point home, specifically referring to Robert Sallow's growth residual.

38:23.100 --> 38:24.740
Ah, the Sallow residual.

38:24.960 --> 38:29.780
It is a classic textbook example of this exact phenomenon, just in a completely different field.

38:29.880 --> 38:31.200
Here's where it gets really interesting.

38:31.340 --> 38:32.060
Tell us about Sallow.

38:32.060 --> 38:35.060
So in the 1950s, the global economy was booming.

38:35.320 --> 38:40.540
And economic models were trying to explain where all this massive economic growth was coming from.

38:40.640 --> 38:40.880
Okay.

38:40.880 --> 38:46.200
The standard models at the time tried to explain economic growth purely through two molar variables.

38:46.380 --> 38:46.680
Toll boxes.

38:46.880 --> 38:47.040
Right.

38:47.580 --> 38:55.860
Increases in capital-like, building more machines, factories, and infrastructure, and increases in labor, simply adding more workers to the population.

38:56.220 --> 38:57.860
Which honestly makes sense.

38:58.200 --> 39:01.720
More machines plus more people should equal more stuff produced.

39:02.060 --> 39:03.280
It's very intuitive.

39:03.280 --> 39:10.760
But when Sallow and others actually ran the numbers on the historical economic data of the 20th century, they found a massive problem.

39:10.760 --> 39:11.720
Let me guess.

39:11.720 --> 39:12.960
The math didn't work.

39:12.960 --> 39:14.240
It completely failed.

39:14.240 --> 39:14.620
Yeah.

39:14.620 --> 39:21.000
The increases in capital and labor could only account for a fraction of the actual economic growth they were observing.

39:21.000 --> 39:23.160
So where did the rest come from?

39:23.160 --> 39:28.020
Well, there was a massive amount of growth left over that those two variables just couldn't account for.

39:28.180 --> 39:29.180
A huge residual.

39:29.540 --> 39:30.480
A massive epsilon.

39:30.780 --> 39:31.040
Yes.

39:31.580 --> 39:33.280
The macroeconomic junk drawer.

39:33.740 --> 39:34.620
That is so funny.

39:34.680 --> 39:35.400
So what did they do?

39:35.900 --> 39:42.160
Initially, economists just labeled that residual technological progress as a sort of hand-wavy catch-all term.

39:42.360 --> 39:44.980
Or they assumed it was mathematical noise in the data collection.

39:45.040 --> 39:45.880
Just throw it in the drawer.

39:45.880 --> 40:00.140
But over the ensuing decades, economists slowly realized that this massive, supposedly unstructured residual was actually hiding incredibly complex, highly structured societal factors.

40:00.500 --> 40:01.040
Like what?

40:01.420 --> 40:02.800
It was hiding human capital.

40:03.420 --> 40:06.700
The education, the health, and the skill levels of the workers.

40:06.920 --> 40:11.060
Because a healthy, educated worker is more productive than a sick, uneducated one.

40:11.060 --> 40:11.500
Exactly.

40:11.760 --> 40:14.040
But the old model just counted them both as one worker.

40:14.240 --> 40:14.580
Wow.

40:14.580 --> 40:19.120
It was hiding institutional quality, like property rights and the rule of law.

40:19.440 --> 40:26.820
It was hiding knowledge spillovers, where one company's innovation accidentally makes another company in a different sector more efficient.

40:27.060 --> 40:28.900
The parallel is almost too perfect.

40:29.380 --> 40:39.880
In both economics and agriculture, the formal, highly respected mathematical model literally hid the most important dynamic parts of the system from the very people trying to optimize it.

40:39.880 --> 40:41.100
It's the ultimate blind spot.

40:41.100 --> 40:47.580
Plant breeders spent a century trying to breed better, more resilient plants while accidentally wearing mathematical blinders.

40:47.780 --> 40:53.420
They were trying to optimize a complex biological engine while stubbornly ignoring half of the actual machinery.

40:53.800 --> 40:56.480
And that is the ultimate tragedy of overcoating.

40:57.280 --> 41:00.920
The mathematical framework hid the microbiome from itself.

41:01.020 --> 41:01.420
Yeah.

41:01.420 --> 41:11.140
And it hid the holobion from the scientists trying to improve it by stubbornly insisting that the isolated molar organism was the only scientifically valid thing to see.

41:11.480 --> 41:13.900
Okay, so we've sufficiently broken the old model.

41:14.160 --> 41:15.420
I think we can agree on that.

41:15.480 --> 41:16.820
It's thoroughly dismantled.

41:16.880 --> 41:24.940
We've admitted that our boundaries were fake, our molar boxes were too small, and our random noise was actually a hidden structure just trying to get our attention.

41:24.940 --> 41:31.440
We have to accept that we are living in a blurry, entangled, molecular, hellabiant world.

41:31.560 --> 41:32.040
We are.

41:32.480 --> 41:35.540
So how do we actually map this reality?

41:35.740 --> 41:41.160
How do we do rigorous predictive science without relying on those rigid molar boxes?

41:41.460 --> 41:42.960
We still need to grow food, right?

41:43.020 --> 41:43.280
We do.

41:43.720 --> 41:52.180
And this is where Fliction does some intense heavy lifting in the mathematical appendices of the paper, specifically appendices B, C, and D.

41:52.260 --> 41:53.560
Oh boy, the appendices.

41:53.560 --> 41:57.740
They propose that to fix this, we can't just tweak the old equation.

41:58.160 --> 42:00.080
We can't just add an M and call it a day.

42:00.100 --> 42:00.980
We have to go deeper.

42:01.480 --> 42:08.440
We have to entirely rethink what a phenotype even is at a fundamental geometric level.

42:08.560 --> 42:09.340
Geometric level.

42:09.500 --> 42:09.840
Yes.

42:10.040 --> 42:10.400
Yes.

42:10.580 --> 42:15.740
We have to move away from looking at single data points and start looking at fields and trajectory basins.

42:15.820 --> 42:22.040
Okay, I am going to need you to be my translator for these appendices because I am definitely not a theoretical mathematician.

42:22.040 --> 42:26.380
Looking at the math in Appendix B, it just looks like a foreign language to me.

42:26.860 --> 42:29.540
What in the world is a trajectory basin?

42:30.000 --> 42:30.800
It's a great concept.

42:30.980 --> 42:34.500
Let's contrast it with the old way of thinking first, which is much easier to grasp.

42:34.620 --> 42:34.860
Okay.

42:34.860 --> 42:41.080
In the classical G by E model, a phenotype is viewed as a single static outcome.

42:41.400 --> 42:43.040
It is a single point in time.

42:43.140 --> 42:44.040
A dot on a graph.

42:44.240 --> 42:44.520
Right.

42:44.880 --> 42:52.320
A breeder looks at a field and says, this specific plant at the end of the harvest season yielded exactly 10 pounds of corn.

42:52.320 --> 42:54.900
That yield is a single point on a graph.

42:55.080 --> 42:55.100
Right.

42:55.240 --> 42:55.960
It's simple.

42:56.140 --> 42:56.760
It's measurable.

42:57.040 --> 42:57.780
You measure the corn.

42:57.900 --> 42:58.480
You weigh the corn.

42:58.580 --> 42:59.260
You log the number.

42:59.400 --> 42:59.720
You're done.

42:59.960 --> 43:06.360
But Appendix B argues that if we take the ecological assemblage, the Hall of Ions, seriously, a phenotype is not a single point.

43:06.500 --> 43:07.140
What is it then?

43:07.380 --> 43:10.000
It is a basin of admissible trajectories.

43:10.220 --> 43:12.500
A basin of admissible trajectories.

43:12.640 --> 43:12.840
Okay.

43:12.980 --> 43:14.160
Let's try to visualize that.

43:14.460 --> 43:16.800
What does a basin look like in this context?

43:16.800 --> 43:22.320
Think of a physical landscape with hills and valleys, like a topographical map you'd use for hiking.

43:22.560 --> 43:22.780
Okay.

43:22.920 --> 43:23.540
I can see that.

43:23.900 --> 43:25.020
The basin is the valley.

43:25.740 --> 43:37.940
In this mathematical model, that valley represents the entire range of possible viable survival paths a plant could take over time, given all the complex constraints acting upon it.

43:38.020 --> 43:42.240
So we aren't just asking, did it yield 10 pounds at the end of the year?

43:42.240 --> 43:49.200
We are asking, how many different ways can this plant successfully navigate absolutely everything nature throws at it over the next six months?

43:49.340 --> 43:49.600
Yes.

43:50.020 --> 43:53.020
It's about mapping the geometry of survival itself.

43:53.640 --> 43:58.940
And here is where the microbiome's true role becomes completely clear mathematically.

43:59.120 --> 44:00.840
How does the microbiome fit into the valley?

44:01.280 --> 44:07.440
A highly beneficial microbiome doesn't just mechanically bump up the yield number at the end of the season like adding synthetic fertilizer does.

44:07.500 --> 44:07.700
Okay.

44:07.820 --> 44:10.880
It actually changes the geometry of that landscape basin.

44:10.880 --> 44:12.640
It changes the shape of the valley.

44:13.020 --> 44:13.280
Yes.

44:13.840 --> 44:14.900
It widens the valley.

44:15.400 --> 44:19.600
It increases the volume of the space of possible futures where the plant survives.

44:19.720 --> 44:20.080
Yeah, I see.

44:20.520 --> 44:32.580
So a good microbial community gives the plant a new biological pathway to survive a sudden two-week drought, for example, or it opens up a chemical pathway to resist a sudden fungal blight that rolls in.

44:32.660 --> 44:33.120
Precisely.

44:33.440 --> 44:35.920
It expands the plant's options for survival.

44:36.160 --> 44:37.100
It gives it wiggle room.

44:37.460 --> 44:37.840
Exactly.

44:38.060 --> 44:38.220
Yeah.

44:38.220 --> 44:45.880
Conversely, a detrimental microbiome or a sterile soil environment where all the microbes have been killed off contracts the basin.

44:46.060 --> 44:46.980
It narrows the valley.

44:47.120 --> 44:47.360
Yes.

44:47.560 --> 44:48.120
It narrows it.

44:48.360 --> 44:58.580
It pushes the plant closer to the edge of the cliff, mathematically speaking, where any slight environmental stress a few days without rain will push it out of the basin and into failure.

44:58.680 --> 44:59.460
It becomes brittle.

44:59.820 --> 45:00.320
Very brittle.

45:00.320 --> 45:05.460
So under this new geometric framing, the entire goal of a breeding program totally changes.

45:05.660 --> 45:06.780
It has to change fundamentally.

45:06.980 --> 45:18.240
If you're only optimizing for instantaneous yield, that single highest point at the end of the season, you might inadvertently select a plant that has a very narrow, steep, brittle trajectory basin.

45:18.360 --> 45:19.260
A prima donna plant.

45:19.260 --> 45:19.780
Exactly.

45:20.080 --> 45:27.920
It only performs perfectly if it gets exactly two inches of rain and the temperature says exactly at 75 degrees and you spray it with exact chemicals.

45:28.180 --> 45:28.300
Right.

45:28.300 --> 45:36.700
But if we connect this to the bigger picture of climate change and shifting weather patterns around the globe, the goal shouldn't be peak yield.

45:36.840 --> 45:39.000
The goal should be robustness.

45:39.220 --> 45:39.740
Robustness.

45:39.740 --> 45:44.020
We should be selecting for the stability of the whole accessible region under stress.

45:44.300 --> 45:51.420
We want plants that preserve viable trajectories across a wide, unpredictable range of ecological perturbations.

45:51.420 --> 45:53.500
Okay, that makes deep, intuitive sense.

45:53.700 --> 45:59.680
We are selecting for resilience and flexibility, not just peak performance in a sterile, perfect laboratory condition.

45:59.760 --> 46:00.140
Exactly.

46:00.380 --> 46:06.180
But how do we actually map the real-time interaction of the plant and the microbiome to achieve that resilience?

46:06.980 --> 46:09.440
Because they are constantly moving targets, right?

46:09.500 --> 46:10.080
They are.

46:10.300 --> 46:15.040
And that brings us to Appendix C, which introduces a concept called the RSVP field theory.

46:15.320 --> 46:18.040
Yes, the Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum Framework.

46:18.160 --> 46:18.340
Yeah.

46:18.340 --> 46:23.780
This is perhaps the most radical, mathematical, and conceptual shift proposed in the entire essay.

46:24.300 --> 46:24.860
RSVP.

46:25.420 --> 46:29.700
It sounds like an invitation to a very complicated, very nerdy party.

46:30.340 --> 46:31.180
Let's break this down.

46:31.600 --> 46:36.720
It is a profound way to visualize how biology actually operates in space and time.

46:36.800 --> 46:37.980
Okay, set the stage for us.

46:38.140 --> 46:42.600
The classical molar model says phenotype equals G plus E plus M.

46:42.600 --> 46:51.360
It treats genotype, environment, and microbiome as independent static variables that you just add together in a linear equation.

46:51.460 --> 46:51.600
Right.

46:51.820 --> 46:52.380
Similar addition.

46:52.720 --> 46:54.540
But in reality, they don't act independently.

46:55.020 --> 46:59.020
They are constantly altering each other continuously at every second of the day.

46:59.120 --> 47:03.760
So how does the RSVP framework handle that constant shifting entanglement?

47:03.760 --> 47:12.360
Instead of additive variables, it treats the entire plant-soil microbe system as a unified, evolving physical field governed by three interacting components.

47:12.560 --> 47:14.200
The S, V, and P.

47:14.360 --> 47:14.540
Right.

47:14.660 --> 47:15.600
First, the scalar field.

47:16.080 --> 47:19.040
This represents the physical distribution of resources in space.

47:19.160 --> 47:20.760
Okay, so where the stuff actually is.

47:20.840 --> 47:21.060
Yes.

47:21.560 --> 47:25.440
Where the water is located in the soil, where the pockets of nitrogen are, where the carbon deposits are.

47:25.460 --> 47:25.620
Okay.

47:25.780 --> 47:27.040
Scalar equals the physical stuff.

47:27.120 --> 47:28.000
The map of resources.

